# Keep wsa881x speaker amps out of runtime PM.
#
# HW-confirmed fix on the MANGMI Pocket Max (SM8250), 2026-08-10. Without it that
# board has NO audio at all — not just no speakers.
#
# Why. The Pocket Max wires BOTH amps to one powerdown line
# (kernel/dts/qcom/sm8250-mangmi-pocket-max.dts: `powerdown-gpios = <&tlmm 127 ...>`
# on speaker@0,1 AND speaker@0,2). Every other wsa881x board here gives each amp its
# own pin — Retroid Pocket uses 129 and 127. Sharing only works at all because
# kernel/patches/0202 requests the GPIO with GPIOD_FLAGS_BIT_NONEXCLUSIVE, which
# makes gpiolib hand each consumer a `gpiolib_shared` proxy chip over the one line.
#
# A shared line aggregates its consumers, so ONE amp left in runtime-suspend keeps
# the line asserted and holds the OTHER amp in powerdown too. The stuck amp then
# never completes SoundWire enumeration:
#     wsa881x-codec sdw:1:0:0217:2010:00:1: Initialization not complete, timed out
#     WSA Playback: ASoC error (-110): at __soc_pcm_open()
# (the timeout is wsa881x_runtime_resume() waiting on the SoundWire core's
# initialization_complete), and the whole card is unusable.
#
# Pinning both amps active from bind keeps the line de-asserted. Verified on the
# device by the debugfs line flipping `out lo` -> `out hi` on an ACTIVE_LOW gpio,
# both amps reaching runtime_status=active, and every wsa881x timeout disappearing
# from the kernel log.
#
# Scope. Matched on the wsa881x SoundWire part ID (0217:2010), so it is inert on
# every board we already ship: SM8550/SM8650 use wsa884x, which is 0217:0204. It
# DOES also catch the four Retroid Pocket boards, which have separate pins and so
# should not need it; there it is belt-and-braces at the cost of leaving the amps
# powered. Narrow it to the Pocket Max if that idle draw ever shows up in a
# measurement — as of now no Retroid board has been powered on to measure.
ACTION=="bind", SUBSYSTEM=="soundwire", KERNEL=="sdw:*:0217:2010:*", ATTR{power/control}="on"
